    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The non-medical use of methamphetamine (MA) is a growing public health problem in the United States. MA use causes devastating behavioral and physiological changes in humans and experimental animal models. Previous studies have shown that hippocampus-associated reward circuitry, such as the dopaminergic pathways and serotonergic pathways, plays an important role in drug addiction. It has further been suggested that the hippocampal formation is responsible for the strong associative memory linked between the drug-induced rewarding effect and the spatial context during both a drug-taking behavior and relapse. Little is known, however, about how the neural substrates of context and spatial information are directly altered during drug-taking behavior. In the current study, we investigated the direct effects of MA on neuronal activities and spatial representations in the medial entorhinal cortex (mEC), a brain structure that is fundamental to an animal’s spatial cognition and learning. To map the anatomical location of MA-activated neurons in the mEC, we employed a Fos-iCreER (TRAP2) transgenic mouse line to genetically tag neurons that were activated after acute MA exposure, under the control of the c-Fos immediate early gene. We found c-Fos-expressing neurons significantly increased in a laminar specific manner in the mEC of MA-treated mice when compared to saline treated controls. More specifically, while layers 2 and 3 of the mEC showed a prominent increase in MA-induced neuronal activation, layer 5 did not. To further investigate MA-induced physiological and cognitive changes in mEC neurons, we trained mice to run on a virtual reality linear track while using a Neuropixel probe to perform high-density electrophysiological recordings. We found that MA significantly suppressed the firing rate of all neurons as well as spatially tuned cells recorded from the mEC. These spatial cells also demonstrated a strong remapping in their spatial representations and a disruption in their speed tuning after MA injection. Together, MA heavily alters the anatomical activation pattern of mEC neurons and further changes the physiological function and spatial cognition that are important for animals’ spatial navigation and self-motion.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
